nst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_operator_45_notequals}{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF ID_stdlib_45_operator_45_notequals}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1440\sl286\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_operator_45_lt}{\fldrslt A.2.9 \f2 operator <(left: int, right: int) returns bool}}\tqr\tldot\tx8640\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_operator_45_lt}{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF ID_stdlib_45_operator_45_lt}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1440\sl286\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_operator_45_gt}{\fldrslt A.2.10 \f2 operator >(left: int, right: int) returns bool}}\tqr\tldot\tx8640\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_operator_45_gt}{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF ID_stdlib_45_operator_45_gt}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1440\sl286\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_operator_45_lte}{\fldrslt A.2.11 \f2 operator <=(left: int, right: int) returns bool}}\tqr\tldot\tx8640\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_operator_45_lte}{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF ID_stdlib_45_operator_45_lte}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1440\sl286\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_operator_45_gte}{\fldrslt A.2.12 \f2 operator >=(left: int, right: int) returns bool}}\tqr\tldot\tx8640\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_operator_45_gte}{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF ID_stdlib_45_operator_45_gte}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\pard\li960\sl286\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_functions}{\fldrslt A.3 Functions}}\tqr\tldot\tx8640\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_functions}{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF ID_stdlib_45_functions}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1440\sl286\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_function_45_minus}{\fldrslt A.3.1 \f2 function -(right: int) returns int}}\tqr\tldot\tx8640\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_function_45_minus}{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF ID_stdlib_45_function_45_minus}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1440\sl286\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_function_45_plus}{\fldrslt A.3.2 \f2 function +(right: int) returns int}}\tqr\tldot\tx8640\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_function_45_plus}{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF ID_stdlib_45_function_45_plus}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\pard\li1440\sl286\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_function_45_abs}{\fldrslt A.3.3 \f2 function abs(x: int) returns int}}\tqr\tldot\tx8640\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_stdlib_45_function_45_abs}{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF ID_stdlib_45_function_45_abs}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb48\li480\sl286\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_appendix_45_bootstrap}{\fldrslt \b B. Bootstrapping the Compiler from C++}}\b \tqr\tldot\tx8640\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_appendix_45_bootstrap}{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF ID_appendix_45_bootstrap}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn2160\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgnlcrm{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4320\tqr\tx8640 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4320\tqr\tx8640 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs22 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4320\tqr\tx8640 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4320\tqr\tx8640 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs22 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb269\s1\sl466 \b\fs35\f1 List of Examples\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb179\li480\sl286\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _488 }{\fldrslt \b0\fs22\f0 4-1. LLVM Prelude (excerpt)}}\b0\fs22\f0 \tqr\tldot\tx8640\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _488 }{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF _488}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\pard\li480\sl286\fi-480 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _491 }{\fldrslt 4-2. JavaScript Prelude (excerpt)}}\tqr\tldot\tx8640\tab {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l _491 }{\fldrslt {\field\flddirty{\*\fldinst PAGEREF _491}{\fldrslt 000}}}}\hyphpar0\par\sect\sectd\plain\pgwsxn12240\pghsxn15840\marglsxn2160\margrsxn1440\margtsxn1440\margbsxn1920\headery0\footery0\pgndec\titlepg{\headerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4320\tqr\tx8640 {}\tab {}\tab {}\par}{\footerf\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4320\tqr\tx8640 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs22 \chpgn }\par}{\headerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4320\tqr\tx8640 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs22 The Scaly Programming Language}\par}{\footerl\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4320\tqr\tx8640 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs22 \chpgn }\par}{\headerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa430\plain\tqc\tx4320\tqr\tx8640 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs22 The Scaly Programming Language}\par}{\footerr\pard\sl-240\sb770\sa910\plain\tqc\tx4320\tqr\tx8640 {}\tab {}\tab {\i\fs22 \chpgn }\par}\pard\sb269\s1\sl466 {\*\bkmkstart ID_introduction}{\*\bkmkend ID_introduction}\b\fs35\f1 1 Introduction\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_intro_45_overview}{\*\bkmkend ID_intro_45_overview}\fs31 1.1 Overview\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Scaly is a programming language designed to be self-scaling: it adapts to the needs of the developer, from simple scripts to complex systems. The language combines a uniform grammar \'97 with no hardcoded operators, since operators are ordinary identifiers \'97 with optional region-based memory management. Standard mathematical precedence applies when operators are chained, while prefix forms and parentheses give explicit control where it is needed.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_intro_45_philosophy}{\*\bkmkend ID_intro_45_philosophy}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 1.2 Design Philosophy\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Scaly follows these core principles:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \i\fs22 Uniform syntax\i0  \'97 Operators are ordinary identifiers rather than keywords baked into the grammar, so the language and its standard library share one small, regular syntax.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \i\fs22 Explicit over implicit\i0  \'97 Memory management and control flow are visible in the code structure.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \i\fs22 Incremental complexity\i0  \'97 Simple programs stay simple; advanced features are available when needed.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_intro_45_status}{\*\bkmkend ID_intro_45_status}\b\fs31\f1 1.3 Implementation Status\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 This specification documents the language as it is implemented. Scaly is built by a self-hosting compiler written in Scaly itself; the compiler compiles its own source and reproduces itself bit-identically across successive bootstrap stages.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 The compiler pipeline \'97 lexer, parser, modeler, planner, and an LLVM-based emitter \'97 is feature-complete. Current implementation status:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \fs22 Lexer, parser, modeler, planner, emitter \'97 \i implemented\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \i0\fs17 \'95 \fs22 LLVM code generation (JIT and ahead-of-time) \'97 \i implemented\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \i0\fs17 \'95 \fs22 Region-based memory management \'97 \i implemented\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \i0\fs17 \'95 \fs22 Self-hosting bootstrap \'97 \i achieved\i0  (the compiler compiles itself)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s1\sl466 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tutorial}{\*\bkmkend ID_tutorial}\b\fs35\f1 2 Tutorial\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb179\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 This chapter is a hands-on introduction to Scaly. It gets you from an empty file to a running program, then takes you on a quick tour of the language. Later chapters cover everything here in depth; the goal now is to write and run real code as fast as possible.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_getting_45_started}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_getting_45_started}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 2.1 Getting Started\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_install}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_install}\fs28 2.1.1 Installing Scaly\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 The quickest way to get the compiler is the installer hosted on scaly.io. Scaly ships as its own LLVM IR (a \i seed\i0 ) and is turned into a native binary on your own machine, so first install LLVM 18 and a C compiler:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 ; macOS\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
brew install llvm@18\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
; Ubuntu / Debian\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
sudo apt install llvm-18 clang\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Then run the installer. It downloads Scaly, builds it for your machine, and puts \b scalyc\b0  on your PATH under \f2 ~/.scaly\f0  \'97 no \b sudo\b0  required:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 curl -fsSL https://scaly.io/install.sh | sh\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Open a new terminal afterwards so the updated PATH takes effect; then \b scalyc\b0  works from any directory, as the rest of this tutorial assumes. To uninstall, remove \f2 ~/.scaly\f0  and the PATH line the installer added to your shell profile.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_build}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_build}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 2.1.2 Building from Source\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 If you have the source repository \'97 for contributing to the compiler itself \'97 you can build \b scalyc\b0  directly instead of using the installer. Scaly is self-hosted: the compiler is written in Scaly and compiles itself, distributed as a \i seed\i0  \'97 the compiler emitted as its own LLVM IR, the \f2 .ll\f0  files committed under \f2 seed/\f0 . A single seed serves every 64-bit little-endian target \'97 the IR carries no target triple, so the compiler you build from it targets whatever machine it runs on. Turning the seed back into a working \b scalyc\b0  needs only LLVM 18 and a C compiler for the final link; no C++ toolchain is involved.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 With the prerequisites above installed, run the build script from the repository root:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 tools/build-from-seed.sh\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 This compiles the seed with \b llc\b0 , links it into \f2 scalyc/build/scalyc\f0 , and builds the small runtime archive the compiler links your programs against. To put it on your PATH from a repository checkout:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 tools/install.sh\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 This installs a small \b scalyc\b0  wrapper in \f2 /usr/local/bin\f0  (override with BINDIR, e.g. \b\f2 BINDIR="$HOME/.local/bin" tools/install.sh\b0\f0 ) that points back at the compiler in this repository and sets SCALY_HOME so the prelude and standard library are found whatever your current directory is; rebuilding the compiler is picked up automatically with no reinstall. Remove it again with \f2 tools/uninstall.sh\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl243 \fs18\lang1024 NOTE 1 tools/install.sh builds the small runtime archive into a persistent location under the repository, and the installed scalyc rebuilds it automatically if it ever goes missing. If you instead run scalyc/build/scalyc directly without installing, its archive lives at /tmp/libscaly.a, which is cleared on reboot; re-run tools/build-from-seed.sh to rebuild it.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \fs22\lang1033 The C++ bootstrap that originally produced the seed is described in {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_appendix_45_bootstrap}{\fldrslt Appendix B}}; you do not need it for everyday use.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_hello}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_hello}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 2.1.3 Hello, World!\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 A Scaly program is just a file of statements \'97 there is no \f2 main\f0  function to declare and nothing to import. Put this single line in a file called \f2 hello.scaly\f0 :\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 print("Hello, World!")\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Compile it to an executable with \f2 -o\f0 , then run the result:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 scalyc -o hello hello.scaly\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
./hello\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 The program prints:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 Hello, World!\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 That is the whole edit-compile-run loop. Everything that follows is just more interesting things to put between the braces.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_vscode}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_vscode}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 2.2 Using Scaly with VS Code\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 You can drive the whole edit-compile-run-debug loop from Visual Studio Code: a build task to compile the file you are editing, a run task to execute it, and a launch configuration that stops at breakpoints and lets you step through your Scaly source. Debugging uses the DWARF information the compiler emits with the \f2 -g\f0  flag, so breakpoints land on real source lines and scalar local variables show their values.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 Open your project folder as your VS Code workspace and install the \i CodeLLDB\i0  extension (extension id \f2 vadimcn.vscode-lldb\f0 ), which provides the \f2 lldb\f0  debugger type used below. Reload the window afterwards (\f3 Command Palette\f0  > \f3 Developer: Reload Window\f0 ) so the extension activates; if \b F5\b0  reports \cf1 configured debug type 'lldb' is not supported\cf0 , CodeLLDB is not installed or not enabled. The tasks call \b scalyc\b0  by name, so install it on your PATH first (see {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_tut_45_install}{\fldrslt Section 2.1.1}}). If Visual Studio Code was already running when you installed \b scalyc\b0 , restart it so its process picks up the updated PATH \'97 otherwise the tasks will fail to find the compiler.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl243 \fs18\lang1024 NOTE 2 Because the installed scalyc wrapper sets SCALY_HOME for you, the compiler finds the prelude (which defines \f2 print\f0  and friends) and the standard library no matter where your .scaly files live \'97 you do not have to work inside the compiler's own source tree. The tasks set \f2 cwd\f0  to \f2 $\{workspaceFolder\}\f0  only so the compiled executable lands beside your source.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_vscode_45_tasks}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_vscode_45_tasks}\b\fs28\f1 2.2.1 Build and Run Tasks\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Create \f2 .vscode/tasks.json\f0  with a build task that compiles the open file with debug info, and a run task that executes the result:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    "version": "2.0.0",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    "tasks": [\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "label": "scaly: build (debug)",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "type": "shell",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "command": "scalyc",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "args": ["-g", "-o", "$\{fileBasenameNoExtension\}", "$\{file\}"],\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "options": \{ "cwd": "$\{workspaceFolder\}" \},\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "group": \{ "kind": "build", "isDefault": true \},\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "problemMatcher": \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                "owner": "scaly",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                "fileLocation": ["autoDetect", "$\{workspaceFolder\}"],\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                "pattern": \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                    "regexp": "^(.*):(\\\\d+):(\\\\d+):\\\\s+(error|warning):\\\\s+(.*)$",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                    "file": 1, "line": 2, "column": 3,\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                    "severity": 4, "message": 5\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
                \}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            \}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \},\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "label": "scaly: run",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "type": "shell",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "command": "$\{workspaceFolder\}/$\{fileBasenameNoExtension\}",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "options": \{ "cwd": "$\{workspaceFolder\}" \},\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "dependsOn": "scaly: build (debug)"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    ]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Press \b Cmd\b0 +\b Shift\b0 +\b B\b0  (or \b Ctrl\b0 +\b Shift\b0 +\b B\b0 ) to build the file in the active editor. The \f2 problemMatcher\f0  turns any compiler diagnostic into a clickable entry in the Problems panel. Use \f3 Terminal > Run Task\f0  and pick \f3 scaly: run\f0  to build and execute.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_vscode_45_launch}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_vscode_45_launch}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 2.2.2 Launch Configuration\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Create \f2 .vscode/launch.json\f0  so \b F5\b0  builds the open file and starts it under the debugger:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    "version": "0.2.0",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    "configurations": [\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "name": "Debug Scaly file",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "type": "lldb",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "request": "launch",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "preLaunchTask": "scaly: build (debug)",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "program": "$\{workspaceFolder\}/$\{fileBasenameNoExtension\}",\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "args": [],\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            "cwd": "$\{workspaceFolder\}"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        \}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    ]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_vscode_45_breakpoints_45_setting}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_vscode_45_breakpoints_45_setting}\b\fs28\f1 2.2.3 Allowing Breakpoints in Scaly Files\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 By default VS Code only lets you set breakpoints in files whose language it recognizes \i and\i0  for which an installed debugger has declared breakpoint support. Languages like C# ship a VS Code extension that does both, so breakpoints work there out of the box. Scaly has no such extension yet, so \f2 .scaly\f0  files are treated as plain text \'97 clicking the gutter does nothing and no red dot appears.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 Until a Scaly language extension exists, lift that restriction by creating \f2 .vscode/settings.json\f0 :\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    "debug.allowBreakpointsEverywhere": true\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 With this set, breakpoints can be placed in any file, including \f2 .scaly\f0 . (The same effect is available globally via \f3 Command Palette\f0  > \f3 Preferences: Open User Settings (JSON)\f0  if you prefer it for every workspace.)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_vscode_45_debug}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_vscode_45_debug}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 2.2.4 Setting a Breakpoint and Stepping\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Put this program in \f2 area.scaly\f0  at the repository root. It computes a rectangle's area from two mutable bindings \'97 small, but enough to stop and inspect some values:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 var width 8\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
var height 5\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
var area width * height\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
if area = 40\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    print("the area is 40")\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
else\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    print("unexpected area")\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Click in the gutter to the left of the \f2 var area width * height\f0  line to set a breakpoint (a red dot appears), then press \b F5\b0 . The program builds and runs, and execution stops at that line before it executes \'97 at which point \f2 width\f0  and \f2 height\f0  already have values but \f2 area\f0  does not yet.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 With execution paused you can:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \fs22 Read \f2 width\f0  and \f2 height\f0  in the \f3 Variables\f0  pane, or hover them in the editor to see their values.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22 Step one line at a time with \b F10\b0  (\f3 Step Over\f0 ) \'97 after stepping past the breakpoint line, \f2 area\f0  appears with the value \f2 40\f0 . Step into a called function with \b F11\b0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22 Press \b F5\b0  again to continue to the program's end.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \lang1033 Breakpoints, stepping, and variable inspection all rely on the \f2 -g\f0  debug information; without it the program still runs, but the debugger has no source lines to stop on.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_vscode_45_callstack}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_vscode_45_callstack}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 2.2.5 Stepping into Functions and the Call Stack\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Stepping becomes more interesting once a function is involved. \b F10\b0  (\f3 Step Over\f0 ) runs a call as a single step; \b F11\b0  (\f3 Step Into\f0 ) descends into the called function so you can watch it run. Put this program in \f2 rectangle.scaly\f0  at the repository root \'97 it factors the area calculation out into a function:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 function area(w: int, h: int) returns int\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    var result w * h\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    result\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
var width 8\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
var height 5\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
var a area(width, height)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
if a = 40\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    print("the area is 40")\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
else\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    print("unexpected area")\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Set a breakpoint on the \f2 var a area(width, height)\f0  line and press \b F5\b0 . When execution stops there, \f2 width\f0  and \f2 height\f0  show in the \f3 Variables\f0  pane. Now press \b F11\b0  instead of \b F10\b0 : the editor jumps into the body of \f2 area\f0  and stops on \f2 var result w * h\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 The \f3 Call Stack\f0  pane (bottom-left of the Run and Debug view) now lists two frames \'97 \f2 area\f0  on top and \f2 main\f0  below it \'97 showing exactly how you got here. Click a frame to switch the \f3 Variables\f0  pane and the editor to that frame's context: selecting \f2 main\f0  shows \f2 width\f0  and \f2 height\f0  again, while \f2 area\f0  shows its own local \f2 result\f0  (still unassigned at this point, just as \f2 area\f0  was before its line ran in the previous example).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 From inside the function you can keep stepping, press \b Shift\b0 +\b F11\b0  (\f3 Step Out\f0 ) to return to the caller, or \b F5\b0  to continue to the end. The function-and-procedure model is covered in {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_tut_45_functions}{\fldrslt Section 2.3.4}} and {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_tut_45_procedures}{\fldrslt Section 2.3.5}}.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_vscode_45_limits}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_vscode_45_limits}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 2.2.6 What the Debugger Shows\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Debug support tracks the parts of the language that are most useful to inspect today, and is being widened over time. At present:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \fs22 Breakpoints land on top-level statements, bindings, and function bodies.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22 The \f3 Variables\f0  pane shows scalar \f2 var\f0  locals \'97 integers and booleans \'97 for whichever stack frame is selected.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22 Breakpoints and stepping work line-by-line inside loop and conditional bodies, so you can stop on individual statements within a \f2 while\f0 , \f2 for\f0 , \f2 if\f0 , or \f2 choose\f0  body, not just on the enclosing statement.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22 Function parameters, \f2 let\f0  (immutable) bindings, and aggregate values such as structures and strings are not shown yet.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22 The \f3 Watch\f0  view and \f3 Debug Console\f0  evaluate expressions as C/C++ and do not understand Scaly names. To read a value, use the \f3 Variables\f0  pane or hover over the name in the editor.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_tour}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_tour}\b\fs31\f1 2.3 A Tour of Scaly\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 This section sweeps quickly across the language using small, complete programs. If something is not fully explained here, do not worry \'97 every feature is covered in detail in the rest of this specification, and each part of the tour points you to the relevant chapter.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_values}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_values}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 2.3.1 Values and Bindings\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Bind a value to a name with \f2 let\f0 . There is no equals sign \'97 the name is followed directly by its value. Use \f2 var\f0  for a binding you intend to change, and \f2 set\f0  to change it. A semicolon starts a comment.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 let answer 42        ; immutable binding\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
var count 0          ; mutable binding\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
set count: count + 1 ; assignment uses a colon\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 The equals sign \f2 =\f0  is reserved for comparison, so it never gets confused with assignment. See {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_lexical}{\fldrslt Chapter 3}} for the full lexical rules.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_operators}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_operators}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 2.3.2 Operators and Precedence\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Operators are ordinary identifiers, not keywords baked into the grammar. When they are chained, they combine using standard mathematical precedence \'97 multiplication binds tighter than addition:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 ; 3 + 4 * 5 is 3 + 20, which is 23 \'97 not 35\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let result 3 + 4 * 5\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
if result = 23\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    print("got 23")\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
else\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    print("unexpected")\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Parentheses group explicitly, and an operator written before its operand acts as a prefix function (\f2 -x\f0 , \f2 ~flag\f0 ). See {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_expressions}{\fldrslt Chapter 7}} for the complete model.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_control_45_flow}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_control_45_flow}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 2.3.3 Control Flow\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f2 if\f0  chooses between branches. Single-statement branches need no braces; the condition needs no parentheses:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 let n 7\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
if n < 0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    print("negative")\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
else if n = 0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    print("zero")\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
else\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    print("positive")\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033 while\f0  repeats as long as its condition holds. Use braces for a multi-statement body:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 var sum 0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
var i 1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
while i <= 10\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    set sum: sum + i\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    set i: i + 1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
; sum is now 55\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033 for\f0  iterates. \f2 for i in 4\f0  runs \f2 i\f0  from 0 up to \'97 but not including \'97 4:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 var total 0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
for i in 4\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    set total: total + i\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
; total is 0 + 1 + 2 + 3 = 6\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_functions}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_functions}\b\fs28\f1 2.3.4 Functions\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 A function declares its parameter types and return type. The value of the last expression is returned automatically \'97 no \f2 return\f0  keyword is needed for the final result. A single-expression body needs no braces:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 function add(a: int, b: int) returns int\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    a + b\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let s add(3, 4)      ; s is 7\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Functions can call themselves. Here is the classic factorial:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 function factorial(n: int) returns int\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    if n <= 1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    else\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        n * factorial(n - 1)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let f factorial(5)   ; f is 120\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Functions are pure: they read their arguments and return a value, with no side effects. To mutate, use a procedure.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_procedures}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_procedures}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 2.3.5 Procedures and Mutation\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 A \f2 procedure\f0  is like a function but may change its inputs and perform side effects. The distinction is visible at every call site, so a reader always knows which calls can mutate state. See {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_types_45_params_45_mutation}{\fldrslt Section 4.7.2}} for the details.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 define Counter(value: int)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    procedure increment(this)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        set value: value + 1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    function get(this) returns int\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        value\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
var c Counter(0)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
c.increment()\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
c.increment()\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let v c.get()        ; v is 2\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_structs}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_structs}\b\fs28\f1 2.3.6 Structures and Methods\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Define a structure by listing its fields. A structure with public fields gets a constructor that takes them in order. Methods are functions or procedures whose first parameter is \f2 this\f0 :\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 define Point(x: int, y: int)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    function distance_squared(this) returns int\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        this.x * this.x + this.y * this.y\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let p Point(3, 4)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let d p.distance_squared()   ; d is 25\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_types_45_constructors}{\fldrslt \fs22\lang1033\f0 Section 4.8}}\fs22\lang1033\f0  covers constructors, including the page-aware \f2 init#\f0  form used by types that allocate.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_unions}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_unions}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 2.3.7 Unions, Options, and choose\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 A \f2 union\f0  holds one of several variants. Each variant may carry a payload. The most common union is \f2 Option\f0 , which represents a value that may be absent:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 define Option union(Some: int, None)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let present Option.Some(42)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let absent  Option.None\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Match on a union with \f2 choose\f0 . Each \f2 when\f0  clause names a binding, then the variant, then the result; the binding is bound to the variant's payload. An \f2 else\f0  clause handles the remaining variants:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 function or_zero(o: Option) returns int\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    choose o\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        when v: Some\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            v\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        else\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let a or_zero(Option.Some(7))   ; a is 7\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let b or_zero(Option.None)      ; b is 0\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 See {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_types_45_unions}{\fldrslt Section 4.11}} for union layout, the \f2 T?\f0  shorthand, and pattern matching in full.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_tut_45_together}{\*\bkmkend ID_tut_45_together}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 2.4 Putting It Together\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Let us combine the pieces into one small, complete program: a safe division that returns an \f2 Option\f0  instead of crashing on a zero divisor, with the caller deciding what to print.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 Start with the function. It returns \f2 None\f0  when the divisor is zero, and \f2 Some\f0  with the quotient otherwise:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 define Option union(Some: int, None)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
function safe_divide(a: int, b: int) returns Option\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    if b = 0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        Option.None\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    else\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        Option.Some(a / b)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Now call it and react to the result with \f2 choose\f0 . The \f2 Some\f0  arm receives the quotient; the \f2 else\f0  arm handles division by zero:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 let result safe_divide(20, 4)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
choose result\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    when q: Some\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        if q = 5\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            print("20 / 4 = 5")\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        else\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
            print("unexpected quotient")\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    else\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        print("cannot divide by zero")\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Put both pieces in \f2 divide.scaly\f0 , then build and run it:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 scalyc -o divide divide.scaly\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
./divide\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 It prints:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 20 / 4 = 5\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Change the call to \f2 safe_divide(7, 0)\f0  and the same program prints \f2 cannot divide by zero\f0  \'97 the union makes the failure a value you must handle, not a crash you can forget.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 From here, the rest of this specification fills in the details: the {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_lexical}{\fldrslt Chapter 3}} rules, the full {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_expressions}{\fldrslt Chapter 7}} model, and the {\field{\*\fldinst   HYPERLINK  \\l ID_types}{\fldrslt Chapter 4}} system including generics, lifetimes, and region-based memory management.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s1\sl466 {\*\bkmkstart ID_lexical}{\*\bkmkend ID_lexical}\b\fs35\lang1024\f1 3 Lexical Structure\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_lex_45_overview}{\*\bkmkend ID_lex_45_overview}\fs31 3.1 Overview\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 This chapter describes the lexical structure of Scaly: how source text is divided into tokens. The lexer transforms a sequence of characters into a sequence of tokens that the parser then processes.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_lex_45_whitespace}{\*\bkmkend ID_lex_45_whitespace}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 3.2 Whitespace and Line Breaks\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Whitespace characters (space, tab) separate tokens but are otherwise insignificant. Line breaks, however, are syntactically meaningful in Scaly \'97 they can terminate statements and declarations.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 A colon (\f2 :\f0 ) can be used in place of a line break when multiple statements should appear on a single line.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_lex_45_comments}{\*\bkmkend ID_lex_45_comments}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 3.3 Comments\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Scaly supports two forms of comments:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \i\fs22 Single-line comments\i0  start with a semicolon (\f2 ;\f0 ) and extend to the end of the line.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \i\fs22 Multi-line comments\i0  start with \f2 ;*\f0  and end with \f2 *;\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
; This is a single-line comment\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
;* This is a\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
   multi-line comment *;\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_lex_45_literals}{\*\bkmkend ID_lex_45_literals}\b\fs31\f1 3.4 Literals\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_lex_45_integers}{\*\bkmkend ID_lex_45_integers}\fs28 3.4.1 Integer Literals\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Integer literals represent whole numbers. They can be written in decimal or hexadecimal notation.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
42        ; decimal\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
0x2A      ; hexadecimal (same value)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_lex_45_floats}{\*\bkmkend ID_lex_45_floats}\b\fs28\f1 3.4.2 Floating-Point Literals\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Floating-point literals represent decimal numbers with a fractional part or an exponent.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
3.14159       ; pi\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
2.5e10        ; scientific notation\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
1.0e-5        ; small number\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_lex_45_strings}{\*\bkmkend ID_lex_45_strings}\b\fs28\f1 3.4.3 String Literals\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 String literals are enclosed in double quotes. They support standard escape sequences.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
"Hello, World!"\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
"Line 1\\nLine 2"      ; with newline escape\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
"Tab:\\tValue"         ; with tab\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
"Quote: \\"nested\\""   ; escaped quotes\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Strings can span multiple lines without any special syntax \'97 newlines are simply included as part of the string content:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
"This is a string\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
that spans multiple\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
lines."\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_lex_45_characters}{\*\bkmkend ID_lex_45_characters}\b\fs28\f1 3.4.4 Character Literals\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Character literals represent single Unicode code points, enclosed in backticks.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
`a`           ; lowercase a\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
`\\n`          ; newline character\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
`\\u\{1F600\}`   ; emoji (grinning face)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_lex_45_booleans}{\*\bkmkend ID_lex_45_booleans}\b\fs28\f1 3.4.5 Boolean Literals\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Boolean literals are \f2 true\f0  and \f2 false\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
true\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
false\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_lex_45_operators}{\*\bkmkend ID_lex_45_operators}\b\fs31\f1 3.5 Operators and Special Symbols\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_lex_45_equals}{\*\bkmkend ID_lex_45_equals}\fs28 3.5.1 The Equals Sign\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Unlike many programming languages, Scaly reserves the equals sign (\f2 =\f0 ) exclusively for equality comparison. It is \i not\i0  used for variable declarations or assignments.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
; Equality comparison\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
if x = 42 \{ ... \}         ; tests if x equals 42\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
; Variable declaration (NO equals sign)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let x 42                  ; declares x with value 42\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let y: i32 100            ; with type annotation\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
; Assignment (uses colon)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
set x: 43                 ; assigns 43 to x\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 This design eliminates the classic confusion between assignment and comparison that leads to bugs in C-style languages (\f2 if (x = 42)\f0  vs \f2 if (x == 42)\f0 ).\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl243 \fs18\lang1024 NOTE 3 Because \f2 =\f0  is just an operator like any other, the standard library could define a unary \f2 =\f0  operator that returns its argument unchanged. This would allow the syntax \f2 let x = 42\f0  to parse as \f2 let x (= 42)\f0 , for those who prefer traditional declaration style.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_lex_45_identifiers}{\*\bkmkend ID_lex_45_identifiers}\b\fs31\f1 3.6 Identifiers\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Identifiers name variables, functions, types, and other entities. Scaly supports three forms of identifiers:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \i\fs22 Alphanumeric identifiers\i0  start with a letter and continue with letters, digits, or underscores.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \i\fs22 Operator identifiers\i0  consist of operator characters such as \f2 +\f0 , \f2 -\f0 , \f2 *\f0 , \f2 /\f0 .\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \i\fs22 String identifiers\i0  are enclosed in single quotes and can contain any characters.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
myVariable        ; alphanumeric\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
calculate_total   ; with underscore\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
++                ; operator identifier\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
'my identifier'   ; string identifier (with spaces)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s1\sl466 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types}{\*\bkmkend ID_types}\b\fs35\f1 4 Type System\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_overview}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_overview}\fs31 4.1 Overview\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Scaly currently recognizes a fixed set of primitive types \'97 integers, floats, booleans, and pointer types \'97 which the LLVM-based emitter maps to the corresponding LLVM types. A possible future direction, described in the following sections, is to make the type system fully backend-agnostic, with even the primitive types supplied by a backend-specific prelude rather than built into the compiler.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_intrinsic}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_intrinsic}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 4.2 Intrinsic Types (Future Direction)\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl243 \b0\fs18\f0 NOTE 4 The mechanism described in this section and the two that follow (\i Type Aliases\i0  and \i Backend Preludes\i0 ) is a planned design, not current behavior. Today the compiler knows its primitive types directly and targets LLVM only.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \fs22\lang1033 An \i intrinsic type\i0  is a type whose implementation is provided by the backend, not by Scaly code. Under this design, intrinsic types would be declared using the \f2 intrinsic\f0  keyword:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define i32 intrinsic\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define i64 intrinsic\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define f64 intrinsic\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 The compiler would not interpret these definitions \'97 it would simply record that these types exist and are intrinsic. The backend (Emitter) would be responsible for mapping intrinsic types to the target platform's representation.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 This design would enable Scaly to target any backend:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \i\fs22 LLVM\i0 : \f2 i32\f0 , \f2 i64\f0 , \f2 f32\f0 , \f2 f64\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\f0 \'95 \i\fs22 JavaScript\i0 : \f2 number\f0 , \f2 string\f0 , \f2 boolean\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\f0 \'95 \i\fs22 WebAssembly\i0 : \f2 i32\f0 , \f2 i64\f0 , \f2 f32\f0 , \f2 f64\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\f0 \'95 \i\fs22 JVM\i0 : \f2 int\f0 , \f2 long\f0 , \f2 float\f0 , \f2 double\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_aliases}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_aliases}\b\fs31\f1 4.3 Type Aliases (Future Direction)\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Type aliases provide human-friendly names for intrinsic or compound types:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define bool i1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define char i32       ; Unicode scalar value\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define int i64        ; Platform word size\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define size_t u64\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 The alias and its target are interchangeable \'97 \f2 bool\f0  and \f2 i1\f0  refer to the same type.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_prelude}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_prelude}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 4.4 Backend Preludes (Future Direction)\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Each backend provides a \i prelude\i0  file that defines the intrinsic types and their aliases for that target. The prelude is implicitly loaded before any user code.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286\qc {\*\bkmkstart _488}{\*\bkmkend _488}\b\lang1024 Example 4-1. LLVM Prelude (excerpt)\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl257 \b0\fs19\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
; Intrinsic types (LLVM native)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define i1 intrinsic\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define i8 intrinsic\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define i16 intrinsic\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define i32 intrinsic\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define i64 intrinsic\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define f32 intrinsic\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define f64 intrinsic\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define ptr intrinsic\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
; Human-friendly aliases\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define bool i1\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define char i32\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define int i64\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define size_t u64\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286\qc {\*\bkmkstart _491}{\*\bkmkend _491}\b\fs22\f0 Example 4-2. JavaScript Prelude (excerpt)\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl257 \b0\fs19\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
; Intrinsic types (JavaScript native)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define number intrinsic\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define string intrinsic\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define boolean intrinsic\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
; Aliases for compatibility\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define int number\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define float number\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define bool boolean\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_literals}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_literals}\b\fs31\f1 4.5 Literal Type Inference\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Numeric and other literals do not have an inherent type. Their type is inferred from context:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
function double(x: i32) returns i32 \{ x * 2 \}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
double(42)           ; 42 inferred as i32 from parameter type\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let y: i64 100       ; 100 inferred as i64 from annotation\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let z y + 50         ; 50 inferred as i64 to match y\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 If the type cannot be inferred, the compiler requires an explicit annotation:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let x 42             ; ERROR: cannot infer type for integer literal\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let x: i32 42        ; OK: type explicitly annotated\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Scaly does \i not\i0  support type suffixes on literals (such as \f2 42i32\f0 ). This keeps literals clean and encourages explicit type annotations where they matter.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_references}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_references}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 4.6 References\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 By default, values flow through functions by value: a function parameter gets read-only access to its argument, and a function returns a value.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 To refer to an existing value without copying it, Scaly provides the reference type \f2 ref[T]\f0  \'97 a non-owning, borrowed reference to a \f2 T\f0 . References are used for parameters and for nullable fields, written \f2 ref[T]?\f0 . Like \f2 pointer[T]\f0 , a reference is represented as a machine pointer, but it carries borrow semantics rather than the unchecked, low-level meaning of a raw pointer.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_pointers}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_pointers}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 4.6.1 Low-Level Pointers\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 For low-level implementation of data structures like \f2 Page\f0 , \f2 HashMap\f0 , or \f2 List\f0 , the type \f2 pointer[T]\f0  is available. This is an escape hatch for implementors, not for everyday code.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
; Low-level list node (internal implementation)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define Node[T] \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    data: T\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    next: pointer[Node[T]]    ; raw pointer for linked structure\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_option}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_option}\b\fs28\f1 4.6.2 Nullable Values\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 For nullable values, use \f2 Option[T]\f0  \'97 a proper sum type with \f2 Some(value)\f0  and \f2 None\f0  variants:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
function find(list: List[T], predicate: function(T) returns bool) returns Option[T] \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    ; returns Some(item) if found, None otherwise\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let result find(items, \\x: x > 10)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
choose result\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    when value: Some: process(value)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    else handle_not_found()\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 The compiler optimizes \f2 Option[T]\f0  to a simple nullable pointer \'97 no space overhead for the tag.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_parameters}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_parameters}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 4.7 Parameter Passing\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 All function parameters are \i borrowed\i0  \'97 functions receive read-only access to their arguments. The caller retains ownership.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define Point \{ x: i32, y: i32 \}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
function distance(a: Point, b: Point) returns f64 \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    ; a and b are read-only views\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    ; cannot modify them\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    ...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let origin Point(0, 0)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let target Point(3, 4)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
distance(origin, target)    ; origin and target unchanged\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_params_45_implementation}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_params_45_implementation}\b\fs28\f1 4.7.1 Implementation\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 The implementation of parameter passing depends on the execution context, but the semantics remain identical:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \i\fs22 Same thread/stack\i0 : A pointer is passed. No copying occurs. The function reads through the pointer.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \i\fs22 Different thread/GPU/remote\i0 : The entire data tree is copied to the target execution context. The function still has read-only access \'97 same semantics, different mechanism.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \lang1033 This design means code doesn't change based on where it executes. A function that works locally works identically when distributed.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_params_45_mutation}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_params_45_mutation}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 4.7.2 Mutation via Procedures\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 To modify data, use a \f2 procedure\f0  instead of a function. Procedures can declare parameters as mutable:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
procedure move(p: mutable Point, dx: i32, dy: i32) \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    set p.x: p.x + dx\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    set p.y: p.y + dy\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
var position Point(0, 0)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
move(position, 5, 3)    ; position is now (5, 3)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 The distinction between functions (pure, read-only) and procedures (may mutate) is explicit in the code. Readers immediately know which calls might have side effects.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_constructors}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_constructors}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 4.8 Constructors\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Constructors create instances of types. Scaly provides both implicit and explicit constructors.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_constructors_45_implicit}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_constructors_45_implicit}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 4.8.1 Implicit Constructors\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 If all members of a type are public, an implicit constructor is generated that takes all fields as parameters in declaration order:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define Point \{ x: i32, y: i32 \}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let p Point(10, 20)    ; implicit constructor\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_constructors_45_explicit}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_constructors_45_explicit}\b\fs28\f1 4.8.2 Explicit Constructors\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Use \f2 init\f0  for explicit constructors when you need:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \fs22 Private members (implicit constructor unavailable)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22 Default values for some fields\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22 Different construction signatures\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define Point\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
(\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    x: i32\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    y: i32\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    init(value: i32) \{      ; convenience constructor\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        set this.x: value\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        set this.y: value\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let p1 Point(10, 20)    ; first init\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let p2 Point(5)         ; second init - Point(5, 5)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_constructors_45_this}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_constructors_45_this}\b\fs28\f1 4.8.3 The \f2 this\f1  Prefix\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 The \f2 this.\f0  prefix is optional when unambiguous, but recommended for clarity:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
init(x: i32, y: i32) \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    set this.x: x    ; clear: field x gets parameter x\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    set this.y: y\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 When parameter names shadow field names, \f2 this.\f0  is required to disambiguate. Avoid \f2 set x: x\f0  \'97 it's confusing even if technically resolvable.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_constructors_45_complete}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_constructors_45_complete}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 4.8.4 Complete Initialization\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 All fields must be initialized by any constructor \'97 implicit or explicit. The compiler enforces this. There are no implicit default values (no automatic 0, false, or null):\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define Point \{ x: i32, y: i32 \}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
init(x: i32) \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    set this.x: x\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    ; ERROR: field 'y' not initialized\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_constructors_45_return}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_constructors_45_return}\b\fs28\f1 4.8.5 Constructor Return\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f2 init\f0  implicitly returns \f2 this\f0 , enabling direct binding:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let p Point(10, 20)    ; init returns the new Point\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_constructors_45_lifetime}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_constructors_45_lifetime}\b\fs28\f1 4.8.6 Constructed Value Lifetime\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 The lifetime of a constructed value is inferred from context:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \i\fs22 In a block\i0 : Local lifetime (current block)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \i\fs22 Last statement / return position\i0 : Call lifetime (return page)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \i\fs22 Explicit annotation\i0 : As specified\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
function example() returns Point \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    let temp Point(1, 2)     ; local - dies at block end\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    return Point(3, 4)       ; call - inferred from return position\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_constructors_45_page_45_param}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_constructors_45_page_45_param}\b\fs28\f1 4.8.7 Page-Parameterized Constructors (\f2 init#\f1 )\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Some types are value types (can live on the stack) but need to allocate internal data on a page. The \f2 init#\f0  syntax supports this pattern:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define String(data: pointer[char], length: size_t)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    ; init# takes an implicit page parameter as first argument\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    init#(page, text: pointer[const_char])\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        let len strlen(text)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        set data: page.allocate(len + 1, 1) as pointer[char]\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        memcpy(data, text, len)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        set length: len\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 At call sites, the lifetime modifier determines which page is passed:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \fs22\f2 String#("hello")\f0  \'97 passes caller's page (rp)\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22\f2 String$("world")\f0  \'97 passes local page\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22\f2 String^hashMap("key")\f0  \'97 passes the named page\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \lang1033 The \f2 init#\f0  pattern is distinct from regular heap allocation. The struct itself can live on the stack \'97 only its internal data needs a page. This is ideal for types like String, Array, or other containers where the wrapper is small but the content may be large.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 Note: \f2 init$\f0 , \f2 init^\f0 , and \f2 init!\f0  are reserved for future use and will produce an error.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_closures}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_closures}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 4.9 Closures (Lambdas)\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Closures are anonymous functions defined with backslash syntax:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\\x: x * 2                    ; single parameter\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\\x y: x + y                  ; multiple parameters\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\\: 42                        ; no parameters\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_closures_45_capture}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_closures_45_capture}\b\fs28\f1 4.9.1 Capturing Variables\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Closures can capture variables from their enclosing scope. Captured variables are treated as implicit borrowed parameters \'97 read-only access, same as explicit function parameters:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let multiplier 10\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let scale \\x: x * multiplier    ; captures multiplier (read-only)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
scale(5)    ; returns 50\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_closures_45_pure}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_closures_45_pure}\b\fs28\f1 4.9.2 Closures Are Pure\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Closures cannot mutate captured variables. They are pure like functions, not imperative like procedures:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
var count 0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let bad \\: \{ set count: count + 1 \}   ; ERROR: cannot mutate capture\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_closures_45_lifetime}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_closures_45_lifetime}\b\fs28\f1 4.9.3 Closure Lifetime\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Since captures are borrowed, a closure cannot outlive its captured variables. The compiler enforces this through lifetime checking:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
function makeCounter() returns (function() returns i32) \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    var count 0\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    return \\: count    ; ERROR: closure outlives captured 'count'\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_closures_45_impl}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_closures_45_impl}\b\fs28\f1 4.9.4 Implementation\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Each closure is an anonymous struct containing its captures, with a call method implementing the body. Closures are monomorphized like other generic types.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_errors}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_errors}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 4.10 Error Handling\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Scaly uses explicit error handling. There are no exceptions or stack unwinding \'97 errors are values returned from functions or procedures.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_errors_45_throws}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_errors_45_throws}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 4.10.1 The \f2 throws\f1  Clause\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Functions or procedures that can fail declare their error type with \f2 throws\f0 :\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
procedure parse(input: String) returns AST throws ParseError \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    if invalid(input) \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        throw InvalidSyntax(position, "expected expression")\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    ...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Under the hood, this is equivalent to returning \f2 Result[AST, ParseError]\f0 , but with dedicated syntax for clarity.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_errors_45_single}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_errors_45_single}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 4.10.2 Single Error Type\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 A function can only throw one error type. To represent multiple error kinds, use a union:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define FileError union\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
(\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    NotFound: String\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    PermissionDenied: String\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    IoError: String\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
function readFile(path: String) returns String throws FileError \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    ...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 This ensures a clear error signature and enables the \f2 try\f0 /\f2 when\f0  pattern for handling specific variants.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_errors_45_try}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_errors_45_try}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 4.10.3 The \f2 try\f1 /\f2 when\f1  Pattern\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Handle errors with \f2 try\f0  and \f2 when\f0  clauses:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
try let ast parse(input)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    when e: InvalidSyntax: reportError(e)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    when e: UnexpectedEof: reportError(e)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 If not all error variants are covered, an \f2 else\f0  clause is required:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
try let ast parse(input)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    when e: InvalidSyntax: reportError(e)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    else panic("unhandled error")\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_errors_45_propagate}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_errors_45_propagate}\b\fs28\f1 4.10.4 Error Propagation\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Use \f2 else throw\f0  to re-throw errors to the caller:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
function process(input: String) returns Data throws ParseError \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    try let ast parse(input)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        else throw    ; re-throws ParseError to caller\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    transform(ast)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 When the error type matches exactly, the error propagates automatically. This simplifies deeply nested code like parsers and visitors.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_errors_45_lifetime}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_errors_45_lifetime}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 4.10.5 Error Lifetime\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Thrown values must have thrown lifetime (\f2 !\f0 ). The compiler infers this from throw position. If inference fails, annotate explicitly:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
throw ParseError.InvalidSyntax(pos, msg)!   ; explicit thrown lifetime\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 The caller provides the exception region where the error will be stored.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_unions}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_unions}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 4.11 Union Types\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 A union type (also called sum type or tagged union) can hold one of several variants. Each variant optionally carries a payload of a single type (use a struct for several fields):\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define Rectangle ( width: f64, height: f64 )\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define Shape union\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
(\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    Circle: f64\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    Box: Rectangle\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    Empty\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let s Shape.Circle(5.0)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_unions_45_layout}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_unions_45_layout}\b\fs28\f1 4.11.1 Memory Layout\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 A union is stored as a tag plus the largest variant:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
Shape = \{ tag: u8, data: [size of largest variant] \}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Unions can contain other unions. Nested unions contribute their full size (tag + data) when computing the parent union's size.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_unions_45_choose}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_unions_45_choose}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 4.11.2 Pattern Matching with \f2 choose\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Use \f2 choose\f0 -\f2 when\f0  to match on variants. Each \f2 when\f0  clause names a binding, then the variant, then the result; the binding is bound to the variant's payload:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
choose s\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    when r: Circle: computeCircleArea(r)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    when b: Box: b.width * b.height\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    else 0.0\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 If not all variants are covered, an \f2 else\f0  clause is required:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
choose s: when r: Circle: computeCircleArea(r) else 0.0    ; handles Box and Empty\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_unions_45_option}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_unions_45_option}\b\fs28\f1 4.11.3 Option Type\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f2 Option[T]\f0  is a union for nullable values:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define Option[T] union\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
(\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    Some: T\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    None\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
function find(list: List[T], pred: function(T) returns bool) returns Option[T] \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    ...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
choose find(items, \\x: x > 10)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    when value: Some: process(value)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    else handle_not_found()\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_unions_45_optimization}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_unions_45_optimization}\b\fs28\f1 4.11.4 Option Optimization\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 For non-pointer types, \f2 Option[T]\f0  optimizes to a pointer:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \fs22\f2 None\f0  = null pointer\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22\f2 Some(value)\f0  = pointer to value\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \lang1033 Note that if T is itself a pointer, the Option Optimization leads to a pointer to another pointer which could be null itself.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_generics}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_generics}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 4.12 Generics\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Scaly supports generic types with type parameters in square brackets:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define List[T] \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    ...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
define HashMap[K, V] \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    ...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let numbers List[i32]()\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let names List[String]()\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_generics_45_mono}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_generics_45_mono}\b\fs28\f1 4.12.1 Monomorphization\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Generics are implemented via \i monomorphization\i0 : each concrete instantiation becomes a completely separate type at compile time. \f2 List[i32]\f0  and \f2 List[String]\f0  share no code at runtime \'97 each has its own specialized implementation.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 Benefits:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \fs22 No runtime overhead \'97 no type descriptors or vtables\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22 Full optimization \'97 the compiler sees concrete types\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22 No boxing \'97 primitives stay primitives\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \lang1033 Trade-offs:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17\lang1024 \'95 \fs22 Larger binaries \'97 each instantiation duplicates code\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li286\sl286\fi-286 \fs17 \'95 \fs22 Longer compile times \'97 more code to generate\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_generics_45_mangling}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_generics_45_mangling}\b\fs28\f1 4.12.2 Name Mangling\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 The Planner generates unique mangled names for each instantiation, following Itanium ABI conventions:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
List[i32]         {\f4\'ae} _ZN4ListIiE...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
List[String]      {\f4\'ae} _ZN4ListI6StringE...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
HashMap[String, i32] {\f4\'ae} _ZN7HashMapI6StringiE...\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 These names are compatible with \f2 c++filt\f0  for debugging.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s2\sl413 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_lifetimes}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_lifetimes}\b\fs31\lang1024\f1 4.13 Lifetimes and Memory Regions\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Scaly uses Region-Based Memory Management (RBMM). Instead of garbage collection or manual malloc/free, values are allocated in memory regions (pages) that are deallocated in bulk when their owning scope exits.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_allocation}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_allocation}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 4.13.1 Stack vs Page Allocation\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 The presence or absence of a lifetime suffix on a constructor call determines whether the value is stack-allocated or page-allocated:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \lang1024 No suffix = Stack allocation\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li440\sl286\fi-440 \tx440 \~\tab The value is allocated on the stack frame. Fast, automatic cleanup when the function returns. Cannot outlive the current function.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 Lifetime suffix = Page allocation\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li440\sl286\fi-440 \tx440 \~\tab The value is allocated on a memory page. Can outlive the current block depending on which lifetime is used.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let stack_point Point(10, 20)     ; stack-allocated (by value)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
let page_point Point$(10, 20)     ; page-allocated (local page)\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \fs22\lang1033\f0 Note: The lifetime suffix comes \i before\i0  the constructor parameters, not after. This follows the general pattern: Type + Generics + Lifetime + Parameters.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_lifetime_45_kinds}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_lifetime_45_kinds}\b\fs28\lang1024\f1 4.13.2 Lifetime Kinds\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \b0\fs22\f0 Local (\f2 $\f0 )\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li440\sl286\fi-440 \tx440 \~\tab Value is allocated on a local page that lives until the end of the current block. The page is lazily allocated on first use and automatically deallocated when the block exits.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 Caller (\f2 #\f0 )\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li440\sl286\fi-440 \tx440 \~\tab Value is allocated on the caller's return page. It survives the function return and is managed by the caller. Used for returning heap-allocated values.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 Thrown (\f2 !\f0 )\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li440\sl286\fi-440 \tx440 \~\tab Value is allocated on the exception page. Used for error values that may be thrown and caught by the caller.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 Reference (\f2 ^name\f0 )\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\li440\sl286\fi-440 \tx440 \~\tab Value is allocated on the same page as the named variable. The named variable must itself be page-allocated (not stack-allocated). Used when adding values to collections.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\s3\sl365 {\*\bkmkstart ID_types_45_lifetime_45_local}{\*\bkmkend ID_types_45_lifetime_45_local}\b\fs28\f1 4.13.3 Local Lifetime ($)\keepn\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb165\sl286 \b0\fs22\lang1033\f0 Local lifetime allocates on a page scoped to the current block. The page is lazily allocated (only when needed) and automatically freed when the block exits:\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
function process(input: String) \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    if condition \{\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        let parser Parser$(input)    ; allocated on local page\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
        parser.parse()\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    \}                                 ; page deallocated here\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
    ; parser and its page are gone\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
\}\sa0\par\fi0\sb0
      \hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl286 \b\fs22\lang1033\f0 Important:\b0  Local lifetime (\f2 $\f0 ) is forbidden on return types. A function cannot return a value with local lifetime because the local page is deallocated before the caller receives the value.\hyphpar0\par\pard\sb330\sl257 \fs19\lang1024\f2 \sa0\par\fi0\sb0
